From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 15:19:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11083/11171] Record what the cascade check buys: triage, not
 prevention

First production firing, and the timeline defines the limit. exp6987 landed at
18:58Z with ready_score 0, exp6988 gate-blocked against it at 19:01Z, four more
tasks skipped at 19:03Z, and the hourly dashboard reported the pending cascade at
19:18Z with the dependent annotated "already blocked".

Three minutes between the upstream landing and the dependent blocking. A check
that runs once an hour at dashboard render cannot see that window, and the 18:18Z
run correctly showed nothing because the condition did not yet exist.

So it does not prevent cascades. What it does is convert one from something
reconstructed afterwards by reading five log lines and chasing a gate reference
into a single named line at the next check, with root and dependent count. The
608 cascade cost a full investigation to trace back to exp6942, so that is worth
having, but it is triage rather than a gate.

Prevention would need the same rule at task-launch time in the conductor: before
launching, if the gate's upstream already exists with a failing field, skip and
say so instead of burning three attempts. That changes scheduling, so it is an
operator decision and is recorded as an option rather than taken.

Explicitly not a criticism of the wiring, which is where I asked for it. The
limitation follows from the cadence and not the rule -- the rule fired on a real
root, named the real dependent, and was correctly silent an hour earlier. Its
population line is what made that silence readable rather than ambiguous.

